# Do higher poverty parts of NYC have differential access to early voting sites?

## Executive summary

**Question.** Are higher poverty parts of NYC more likely to be assigned, by election district, to an early voting site that is harder to reach? The unit is a tract district, the overlap of one tract with one election district, which keeps its tract's poverty rate. ({{ r.tract_districts }} tract districts from {{ r.tracts }} tracts, {{ r.poll_sites }} early voting sites.) Access is measured {{ r.measures | length }} ways: {% for m in r.measures %}"{{ m.short | lower }}"{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}. These are variants of one question, not separate studies.

**Answer.** {% if s.supported %}Yes on the primary measure ({{ pm.short | lower }}): higher poverty tract districts are more likely to be assigned {{ pm.phrase }}.{% else %}Not on the primary measure ({{ pm.short | lower }}): higher poverty tract districts are not more likely to be assigned {{ pm.phrase }}.{% if s.opposite_and_significant %} The association runs the opposite way.{% endif %}{% endif %}

- Primary measure, across the five poverty classes: {{ s.first.rate | pct }} of {{ s.first.group | lower }} tract districts are assigned {{ pm.phrase }}, against {{ s.last.rate | pct }} of {{ s.last.group | lower }} tract districts. Chi-squared = {{ "%.2f" | format(c.chi2) }}, p = {{ c.p | p }}, Cramér's V = {{ "%.3f" | format(c.cramers_v) }}, trend test one-sided p = {{ b.trend.p_one_sided | p }}.
- Disparate impact: tract districts at {{ "%.0f" | format(HIGH_POVERTY) }}% poverty or above avoid that outcome {{ di.favorable_rate_high | pct }} of the time, those below {{ di.favorable_rate_lower | pct }}. The impact ratio is {{ "%.2f" | format(di.impact_ratio) }}, which {{ "fails" if di.fails_four_fifths else "passes" }} the four-fifths rule.
- Overall, {% for m in r.measures %}{{ r.overall_rates[m.key] | pct }} of tract districts have "{{ m.short | lower }}"{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}.
- Of the other {{ r.tests | length }} tests (Holm corrected together), {{ s.toward | length }} are significant and consistent with the hypothesis, {{ s.opposite | length }} are significant and contradict it, and {{ s.not_significant }} are not significant. See All other tests.

**Caution.** Neighboring tract districts are not independent, and tract districts of one tract share a poverty rate, so p-values are probably too small. Tract districts are counted equally whatever their size or population. See Limits.

